## Transfer consequence

The loss from a common-coordinate stiffness cycle is now an explicit nonnegative magnitude: \(\Delta F_{\mathrm{ret}}-\Delta F_{\mathrm{full}}=\theta I(x;y\mid z)\ge0\), under this prescribed additive full-space model. A negligible conditional information makes the retained determinant an accurate surrogate for the full stiffness cycle. Zero conditional cross-compliance \(U_w^TD^{-1}V_w=0\) suffices, even if both bundles touch omitted coordinates. It does not establish that the retained Hessians themselves are additive. Joint16's additive-fit test and this determinant-fidelity test answer distinct questions.

The next classical benchmark can report both unconditional and conditional contact information, together with the omitted-coordinate map and uncertainty. A non-negligible conditional term rejects interpreting the retained determinant as the full stiffness cycle at a tighter error tolerance; it does not reject a common-coordinate covariance as a mechanistic diagnostic. The additional compliance involves the conditional block \(D^{-1}\), not simply a principal submatrix of \(K^{-1}\). Sparse factorisations can compute the required responses without molecular dynamics in a prescribed model. P-based estimation would need those responses, correct inverse scaling, valid observables, conditioning and a complete resource comparison; the identity supplies no new quantum algorithm.

